  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Golovinomyces magnicellulatus is a fungal pathogen belonging to the powdery mildew family that affects various plant species throughout New England. This obligate parasite forms characteristic white, powdery coatings on plant leaves and stems, weakening host plants by disrupting photosynthesis.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Large Conidiospores",
      "description": "Distinctive large conidiospores (asexual spores) that are notably larger than those of related powdery mildew species, giving the fungus its 'magnicellulatus' name meaning 'large cells'",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "White Powdery Coating",
      "description": "Forms a fine, easily brushed-off white powder on leaf surfaces, typically beginning on lower leaves and progressing upward",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Cleistothecial Features",
      "description": "Produces dark spherical fruiting bodies (cleistothecia) in late season that contain asci with ascospores for overwintering",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "This fungus thrives in New England's cool, humid conditions and is commonly found on herbaceous and woody plants in gardens, forests, and agricultural areas, particularly during spring and fall when humidity is high.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Unlike many fungi, powdery mildews like G. magnicellulatus actually prefer cooler temperatures and high humidity but do not require free water on leaves to infect plants, making them unique among fungal pathogens.",
